ding"},"teaching":{"steps":["Separate **belief now** from the **event yesterday**.","The event precedes the current belief, so use the perfect infinitive **to have prepared**.","Choose **to have prepared**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The simple perfect infinitive needs a past participle.","B":"The event precedes the current belief, so use the perfect infinitive **to have prepared**.","C":"An infinitive uses base have, not has.","D":"To takes base have, not had."}}},{"id":"d93b038d-180f-5a98-a072-2b13489b02d1","topicSlug":"advanced-passives","level":"C1","prompt":"Someone else did the task for us. Complete the causative: “We had the evening meal ____.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"preparing"},{"id":"B","text":"to prepare"},{"id":"C","text":"prepared"},{"id":"D","text":"prepare"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"The object receives the action, so use a **past participle** after it.","explanation":"**Had + object + prepared** describes the arranged or experienced action on that object.","keywords":["causative-service"],"skill":"causative-service","contextKey":"advanced-passives-49","fingerprint":"3480b7afbc46e383b4d7d8afd5745d0a78bd9a11d0ac3c374413125a5869d9a4","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-advanced-passives"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The object receives the action, so use a **past participle** after it.","**Had + object + prepared** describes the arranged or experienced action on that object.","Choose **prepared**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"An -ing form gives a different construction, not the requested completed service.","B":"Have + object + to-infinitive is not the standard causative pattern here.","C":"**Had + object + prepared** describes the arranged or experienced action on that object.","D":"A bare verb after the object is not this causative have + object + participle pattern."}}},{"id":"a769d9ae-008a-5da2-af0a-aa186e49531a","topicSlug":"advanced-passives","level":"C1","prompt":"Use a perfect passive infinitive: “The evening meal is thought to have ____ already.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"been preparing"},{"id":"B","text":"been prepared"},{"id":"C","text":"being prepared"},{"id":"D","text":"be prepared"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"The thing receives the earlier action: **to have been + past participle**.","explanation":"After to have, **been prepared** makes the infinitive both perfect and passive.","keywords":["perfect-passive-infinitive"],"skill":"perfect-passive-infinitive","contextKey":"advanced-passives-49","fingerprint":"af9021775970431785becfc3981084d2175465ece66506cd206c34b90ad689a3","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-advanced-passives"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The thing receives the earlier action: **to have been + past participle**.","After to have, **been prepared** makes the infinitive both perfect and passive.","Choose **been prepared**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The passive ends in a past participle, not an -ing form.","B":"After to have, **been prepared** makes the infinitive both perfect and passive.","C":"Have requires been, not being.","D":"Base be cannot follow perfect have."}}},{"id":"8a2d97f7-2206-52eb-aceb-0fe50fd3842f","topicSlug":"advanced-passives","level":"C1","prompt":"Report a habitual activity impersonally: “She is believed ____ the rowing boat every week.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repair"},{"id":"B","text":"repaired"},{"id":"C","text":"repairing"},{"id":"D","text":"to repair"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"After **is believed**, use a suitable **to-infinitive**.","explanation":"The reporting passive **is believed** takes **to repair** for this habitual activity.","keywords":["reporting-passive-infinitive"],"skill":"reporting-passive-infinitive","contextKey":"advanced-passives-50","fingerprint":"14717f74b02685b7f8dbefb799246eb8dfd922d3d3f8c7963711e77d6d791721","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-advanced-passives"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["After **is believed**, use a suitable **to-infinitive**.","The reporting passive **is believed** takes **to repair** for this habitual activity.","Choose **to repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The reporting-passive pattern requires a to-infinitive.","B":"A bare past form cannot follow is believed in this construction.","C":"The simple -ing form does not complete this reporting-passive pattern.","D":"The reporting passive **is believed** takes **to repair** for this habitual activity."}}},{"id":"7f21bfec-165d-50f3-ae0c-7ef4f343c759","topicSlug":"advanced-passives","level":"C1","prompt":"The event was yesterday: “She is believed ____ the rowing boat then.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"to has repaired"},{"id":"B","text":"to had repaired"},{"id":"C","text":"to have repairing"},{"id":"D","text":"to have repaired"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Separate **belief now** from the **event yesterday**.","explanation":"The event precedes the current belief, so use the perfect infinitive **to have repaired**.","keywords":["earlier-reported-event"],"skill":"earlier-reported-event","contextKey":"advanced-passives-50","fingerprint":"7ab56eaf4dd1199971d697e8eee41a4de7f0088160f97c5b0bedb5b14c788662","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-advanced-passives"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Separate **belief now** from the **event yesterday**.","The event precedes the current belief, so use the perfect infinitive **to have repaired**.","Choose **to have repaired**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"An infinitive uses base have, not has.","B":"To takes base have, not had.","C":"The simple perfect infinitive needs a past participle.","D":"The event precedes the current belief, so use the perfect infinitive **to have repaired**."}}},{"id":"09964a5a-54ce-5b0e-a564-8ed906364962","topicSlug":"advanced-passives","level":"C1","prompt":"Someone else did the task for us. Complete the causative: “We had the rowing boat ____.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repair"},{"id":"B","text":"repairing"},{"id":"C","text":"to repair"},{"id":"D","text":"repaired"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"The object receives the action, so use a **past participle** after it.","explanation":"**Had + object + repaired** describes the arranged or experienced action on that object.","keywords":["causative-service"],"skill":"causative-service","contextKey":"advanced-passives-50","fingerprint":"af0123a0548894a4f7f43f7225f973c548320d6ff8a325d390ec05606ceecf0a","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-advanced-passives"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The object receives the action, so use a **past participle** after it.","**Had + object + repaired** describes the arranged or experienced action on that object.","Choose **repaired**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A bare verb after the object is not this causative have + object + participle pattern.","B":"An -ing form gives a different construction, not the requested completed service.","C":"Have + object + to-infinitive is not the standard causative pattern here.","D":"**Had + object + repaired** describes the arranged or experienced action on that object."}}},{"id":"7bf304fa-135c-5b26-a961-42abb8dee858","topicSlug":"advanced-passives","level":"C1","prompt":"Use a perfect passive infinitive: “The rowing boat is thought to have ____ already.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"be repaired"},{"id":"B","text":"been repairing"},{"id":"C","text":"been repaired"},{"id":"D","text":"being repaired"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"The thing receives the earlier action: **to have been + past participle**.","explanation":"After to have, **been repaired** makes the infinitive both perfect and passive.","keywords":["perfect-passive-infinitive"],"skill":"perfect-passive-infinitive","contextKey":"advanced-passives-50","fingerprint":"20121c79b8854c458e193cf36ed19ec7387de458c708ae929d46463c2781675f","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-advanced-passives"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The thing receives the earlier action: **to have been + past participle**.","After to have, **been repaired** makes the infinitive both perfect and passive.","Choose **been repaired**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Base be cannot follow perfect have.","B":"The passive ends in a past participle, not an -ing form.","C":"After to have, **been repaired** makes the infinitive both perfect and passive.","D":"Have requires been, not being."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Report beliefs impersonally, combine passive with perfect forms, and describe services someone arranges.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Impersonal reporting","explanation":"A reporting passive can avoid naming who holds a view: **It is believed that ...**. Another pattern makes the reported subject the main subject: **She is believed to ...**. These constructions report a claim; they do not establish that it is true.","pattern":"It is believed that she lives here. / She is believed **to live** here.","commonMistakes":["Do not confuse the time of the belief with the time of the event being reported."],"examples":["Report a habitual activity impersonally: “She is believed **to prepare** the visitor guide every week.”","Report a habitual activity impersonally: “She is believed **to restore** the old portrait every week.”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Earlier events after a reporting passive","explanation":"Use a **perfect infinitive** when the reported event is earlier than the reporting reference: is believed to have left. A continuous infinitive can show an ongoing activity: is thought to be working. A passive infinitive adds be or have been before the participle.","pattern":"The bridge is thought **to have been built** in 1900.","commonMistakes":["To have built is active; to have been built is passive. The participant’s role determines which is needed."],"examples":["The event was yesterday: “She is believed **to have prepared** the visitor guide then.”","The event was yesterday: “She is believed **to have restored** the old portrait then.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Arrange for someone else to do something","explanation":"**Have / get + object + past participle** often describes a service or arranged action. The subject arranges or experiences the action rather than necessarily performing it. Get is often less formal. The causative verb carries tense.","pattern":"We **had the roof repaired**.","commonMistakes":["We had repaired the roof is past perfect active and normally says we did the repair; it is a different structure."],"examples":["Someone else did the task for us. Complete the causative: “We had the visitor guide **prepared**.”","Someone else did the task for us. Complete the causative: “We had the old portrait **restored**.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Get-passives and affected experiences","explanation":"Get + participle can form an informal passive, especially for events or changes affecting someone. Causative have can also describe an unwelcome experience that was not arranged, such as having a bag stolen. Meaning and context matter.","pattern":"The parcel **got damaged**. / She **had her bag stolen**.","commonMistakes":["Have something done does not always imply the subject chose or paid for the action."],"examples":["Use a perfect passive infinitive: “The visitor guide is thought to have **been prepared** already.”","Use a perfect passive infinitive: “The old portrait is thought to have **been restored** already.”"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["It is believed that she lives here. / She is believed **to live** here.","The bridge is thought **to have been built** in 1900.","We **had the roof repaired**.","The parcel **got damaged**. / She **had her bag stolen**."]},"stage":"6 · Advanced control","prerequisites":["agreement-advanced"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
